#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275383 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "100da246-a4f5-543a-91d0-e6d3a1f36af7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272530} {:answer/id #uuid "3f886435-adcb-5d14-a0ea-d8238504ef8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272932} {:answer/id #uuid "74151cc2-d101-54b9-b1d6-6503644e7ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273380} {:answer/id #uuid "8e9e7313-37e7-5fd3-8c10-525805d2da7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273622} {:answer/id #uuid "ee688365-d420-5470-af23-7bd64976045b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f886435-adcb-5d14-a0ea-d8238504ef8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272932} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8437634-a0b8-5fc4-8ac1-18de47b710d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "40a86374-09c1-5fcd-a58c-a5f678fb3c1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-157228" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $-{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}+5{a}_{3}=0.$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly dependent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}+5{a}_{3} & =-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+5[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2+0+15 \\\\ -7-10+20 \\\\ 0+0+5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 7{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can reorder the equations in this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}7{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is true, the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275384 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04fd0e5f-aec1-5e47-9884-6914dcdde436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272432} {:answer/id #uuid "53f1398f-c03c-5a8d-aa1c-eb0e6f8cff38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273102} {:answer/id #uuid "ac422526-fbb5-5674-b3b5-76631c070aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273873} {:answer/id #uuid "ceac787a-b79a-5630-828b-66afa1cf3251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274184} {:answer/id #uuid "fc49f684-7f45-59ee-80e0-cb6b1d9c3f4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274604}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac422526-fbb5-5674-b3b5-76631c070aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273873} :answer-field/id #uuid "2df80ac5-b285-5b02-8dca-ab5fb0ce1e6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2473ef72-1c36-57f9-bdd5-12c0b04c9229" :question/math-academy-id "q-157236" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{1}-3{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=0.$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}-3{a}_{2}+{a}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1-3+4 \\\\ 5-6+1 \\\\ 2-12+10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since statement I is true, we have ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ for ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}=1$ and ${x}_{2}=-3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly *dependent*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275385 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5006f768-11c3-5ac0-90d9-2162bfc97a5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273064} {:answer/id #uuid "5263eeac-acf6-5632-a072-db27258fb19b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273086} {:answer/id #uuid "937fea17-57d4-5481-a16d-85b3d97403cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273670} {:answer/id #uuid "974dcb42-b48b-55d7-b6e7-347d8f0017c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273694} {:answer/id #uuid "e2766448-047c-5fd7-b437-d5019c276623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2766448-047c-5fd7-b437-d5019c276623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274391} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1a267d3-2a63-5b80-a712-24bba65938c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a541a49-3d67-515c-a6d4-f4d315e78809" :question/math-academy-id "q-157238" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3}=0.$\n- ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-8+6 \\\\ -1+10-9 \\\\ 7+2-9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since statement I is true, we have ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ for ${x}_{1}=1,\\,{x}_{2}=2$ and ${x}_{3}=3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly *dependent*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c1ef73b-2200-5136-bd84-1316fee9ec86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272630} {:answer/id #uuid "4fb7209e-b9ac-58a5-b5a5-a4fc2e434c0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273057} {:answer/id #uuid "89bd5d8a-93c5-5b37-8741-1f23fb8650c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273575} {:answer/id #uuid "c3719fdb-b4af-5565-aa73-c0e272a87f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274083} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f2772a-9789-5fb4-9457-8af14d6679bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89bd5d8a-93c5-5b37-8741-1f23fb8650c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273575} :answer-field/id #uuid "40b7da87-c0aa-59da-b014-b40663175ee8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d5864d3-92dc-56c9-ae91-3d7c6bdd2052" :question/math-academy-id "q-157252" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $-6{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=0.$\n- ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}-{a}_{3} & =-6[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12-8-4 \\\\ -6+4+2 \\\\ -12+7+5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since statement I is true, we have ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ for ${x}_{1}=6,\\,{x}_{2}=-1$ and ${x}_{3}=1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly *dependent*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275387 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08b8264d-8430-511e-8cde-be2e4dcc4c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272463} {:answer/id #uuid "3fa1de63-fc47-5c38-a95a-9869cce3ecf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272934} {:answer/id #uuid "667fe6e5-0531-57c5-b543-21e86eb6793e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273267} {:answer/id #uuid "af1a126a-5404-5079-8709-0d8536dfddc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273900} {:answer/id #uuid "f0bfb04d-fa34-53e6-92a3-cf2bbd8ac16e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08b8264d-8430-511e-8cde-be2e4dcc4c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272463} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba4a8d7b-d263-5a48-a8b5-7c3e383d92cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f4d0dbc3-088e-54a3-8420-9efcf1fc89b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-157253" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $-{a}_{1}+3{a}_{2}=0.$\n- ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n- The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{a}_{1}+3{a}_{2} & =-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275388 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00f7fd53-ae8f-5913-86fa-0ae247f81b2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272391} {:answer/id #uuid "2b97de82-74cf-5fce-9fb4-a6089edeffab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272777} {:answer/id #uuid "c56bcc2d-2334-5927-b8e1-7f9d132c7992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274100} {:answer/id #uuid "f2aac8cc-ed6d-5db7-8d0e-49e339eb48b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274531} {:answer/id #uuid "fe933476-895c-55d7-8ca7-435be0c082f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c56bcc2d-2334-5927-b8e1-7f9d132c7992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274100} :answer-field/id #uuid "398e41ba-ab14-5def-8898-bcd7b81bf1c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e89e99e-07af-5529-a0c3-3af7fe4158cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-157256" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0.$\n- ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n- The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =4[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275397 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119928 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f3018fd-9d16-578f-b050-966890844c9b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Plane Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27cc3918-24ec-5e24-8b92-ebb58acf51fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272739} {:answer/id #uuid "8bafd870-91da-5434-980f-ea70e6d7adc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273591} {:answer/id #uuid "aac0ddfe-c23d-51bd-9fd7-43590adfbdae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273863} {:answer/id #uuid "b4ec1e3c-cf2c-5bac-ace5-4eb8c4abbb63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273957} {:answer/id #uuid "c7317138-c784-5196-847f-2aeb2b896bb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bafd870-91da-5434-980f-ea70e6d7adc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273591} :answer-field/id #uuid "857b4872-6e3e-5ebe-80dd-fdf164eec567" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "70407f65-cb23-502b-8caa-ecb6f34e8d13" :question/math-academy-id "q-157468" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $x+y-2z=3$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n- $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n- $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3-y+2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The line does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y-2z & =3 \\\\ (0)+(0)-2(0) & =3 \\\\ 0 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider $a=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3+2 \\\\ 0+1 \\\\ 0+0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ does not belong to $Π$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y-2z & =3 \\\\ (5)+(1)-2(0) & =3 \\\\ 6 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin Π,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275398 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119928 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f3018fd-9d16-578f-b050-966890844c9b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Plane Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31268b47-876b-5491-adab-1fe732b1540e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272826} {:answer/id #uuid "469d3ace-b5e2-503d-9621-c5d54ad53c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272989} {:answer/id #uuid "8e21d640-5ee3-590d-9931-13b6db5cafbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273613} {:answer/id #uuid "ab4c5364-84a4-5300-b610-7febbed55810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273866} {:answer/id #uuid "b841a7b8-2665-5caf-acf1-5fddc3de45c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b841a7b8-2665-5caf-acf1-5fddc3de45c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273982} :answer-field/id #uuid "4098f907-a164-58c9-b6fc-816f9b668cc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274746}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "39240080-5310-56c6-b798-6051cae1cac8" :question/math-academy-id "q-157469" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $x-2y+z=1$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n- $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n- $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1+2y-z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The line does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y+z & =1 \\\\ (0)-2(0)+(0) & =1 \\\\ 0 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1+0 \\\\ 0+0 \\\\ 0+1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ does not belong to $Π$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y+z & =1 \\\\ (1)-2(0)+(1) & =1 \\\\ 2 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin Π,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278201 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f44416f-69e0-507a-8af0-f5bbadd3b258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275899} {:answer/id #uuid "62ac1246-f355-5d5b-9f03-0f23175cfb0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276370} {:answer/id #uuid "9b727b5a-3b9b-51b3-9f78-d4123ed9b037" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276870} {:answer/id #uuid "dcbcf052-be4c-5afc-af18-66153706425e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277410} {:answer/id #uuid "ea596630-12b8-556c-b338-7b819d55d195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b727b5a-3b9b-51b3-9f78-d4123ed9b037" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276870} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcf3d337-d415-56f0-b019-8733e6a44b61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278062}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7714139-ef1f-551b-b703-2555d562d417" :question/math-academy-id "q-158536" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y\\le 2},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=2}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(2,2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(2,2)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(-2,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or below the line $y=2.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/de/de056be6665a342dc8569e0db330743a96e08fa770798c94ec7dd0cc4e987d92.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(2,2)$ and $Q(-2,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/c6/c6c1237dfa5f8fed76bad01466c94442d27825c4edab7dbc92a99b177af4a0b6.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(2,2)$ satisfy the equation $y=2.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(2,2)$ satisfy the inequality $y\\le 2.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. $Q$ is an interior point of $A$ since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is entirely contained in $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278202 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "242d457c-d9d4-5b70-b28f-db71ec258364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275797} {:answer/id #uuid "331e3c71-a8d9-599a-87ff-16e343647d15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275933} {:answer/id #uuid "45e0218b-ee80-5c7d-ae06-bcadb5127373" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276091} {:answer/id #uuid "625679e3-867a-54ab-b04a-2f6cd1f08d05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276364} {:answer/id #uuid "9add89b8-1bd9-5fa1-838d-28cc0b22f8e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "331e3c71-a8d9-599a-87ff-16e343647d15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275933} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d7950a6-3ccf-5b00-8fc7-9050b087f1bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0d5bc0a-69ec-5558-bf1a-6901b6354f4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-158538" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:x\\le 1},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:x=1}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $Q(2,-2)$ is an interior point of $A$\n- The point $P(1,2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(1,2)$ belongs to $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or to the left of the line $x=1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/11/119fbce04df572e5b1c083432d0183da1cc04add5e4ad56ac7704af3cf8ee024.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,2)$ and $Q(2,-2)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/7d/7dc96640505a272ec9701a9d155f7ed2809e6f6df29ac5327af6b2b2324fa162.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(1,2)$ satisfy the equation $x=1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The coordinates of $P(1,2)$ satisfy the inequality $x\\le 1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "712e0e29-53ea-5ea8-bea9-dd15f47daede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276496} {:answer/id #uuid "7416238f-7f09-5e8e-abf1-9e7a12ef6051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276525} {:answer/id #uuid "a7669454-dc40-5b91-88f8-b1a3af07b48c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276986} {:answer/id #uuid "adb8727b-5837-5346-9982-c8978bf46ab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277037} {:answer/id #uuid "be15f5bc-9aba-570c-a865-d79998864d08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "712e0e29-53ea-5ea8-bea9-dd15f47daede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276496} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fb5a8fe-370a-50e7-9adc-4a36ab91e0ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277893}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5cce0d48-0281-56f6-9336-25d5bd51241e" :question/math-academy-id "q-158541" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y\\ge 3x-1},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=3x-1}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(0,-3)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(0,-3)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(-1,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or above the line $y=3x-1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/7d/7df3b0c7e884325519ea15f5f949d4b254ef4f63f2449fd36a7aa811ff1b385f.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(0,-3)$ and $Q(-1,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/7f/7f710e625802403ca5a34f4331cb2998272479c72deca0e24a6116072e4afb5f.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of $P(0,-3)$ do not satisfy the equation $y=3x-1.$ Hence, $P$ is not in $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(0,-3)$ do not satisfy the inequality $y\\ge 3x-1.$ Hence, $P$ is not in $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $Q$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is entirely contained inside the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'III only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278208 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ec8d07b-f074-551e-87b0-be8d07f75c4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275604} {:answer/id #uuid "12ef0e3f-940d-54a6-b879-056c7271a92c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275648} {:answer/id #uuid "14e3062f-1867-5b5c-b9bf-f133948f5e16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275673} {:answer/id #uuid "46146491-4c02-583c-b4da-26afd46b9771" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276095} {:answer/id #uuid "fd6fb0c4-71eb-59a9-a329-b326663c00e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ec8d07b-f074-551e-87b0-be8d07f75c4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275604} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddf8aa29-3ce7-5aee-95bd-b1518a40ff52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14446fa2-dc95-59c6-9ff9-236caf0a3198" :question/math-academy-id "q-158562" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y<1-2x\\},\\,∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=1-2x\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The point $P(-1,3)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n\nII. The point $P(-1,3)$ belongs to $A$\n\nIII. The point $Q(1,-1)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie below the line $y=1-2x.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/7c/7ce1ca6af3caf2b454d7fed484f98d11478839bcf107c0f19562688c7cdaca6a.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(-1,3)$ and $Q(1,-1)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/aa/aad8bc7bfd64af0dd1e08618626ae3237bb4aba42be1f91fe7ff9328ad6ecbe7.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(-1,3)$ satisfy $y=1-2x.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(-1,3)$ do not satisfy the inequality $y<1-2x.$ Hence, $P$ is not in $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $Q$ is *not* an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278218 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fd1891c-95b9-50f7-b0d4-44edb4505415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276033} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9f1302-a225-50dc-8282-9cdc30e460cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276298} {:answer/id #uuid "8a5a4698-2fec-5e90-91df-84219743e4e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276727} {:answer/id #uuid "92236399-f0b6-5795-b074-da5f2cda69c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276800} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc48f93-0f2e-5c19-8450-cae78ea3b874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fd1891c-95b9-50f7-b0d4-44edb4505415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276033} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b8af4d1-9fe9-507f-bcdf-572cdb0ebc23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2253cfff-ee0a-5c15-93af-d26f561ec17f" :question/math-academy-id "q-158603" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:x>1\\},\\,∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:x=1\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The point $P(1,1)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n\nII. The point $P(1,1)$ belongs to $A$\n\nIII. The point $Q(0,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie to the right of the line $x=1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/7d/7dc932c43ed153f4ac1832ca136f9035d879ea1f54e01c69e7a032b7a53d4940.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,1)$ and $Q(0,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/06/06fdba4e26ebad28ef3f63106b7bdd8cb115b91a5f0076370a27b12f05added3.png)\n\n \n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(1,1)$ satisfy $x=1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(1,1)$ do not satisfy the inequality $x>1.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124642 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b91dfb0-ae09-545f-ac7a-c0d00abc2f58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275733} {:answer/id #uuid "5e409313-241a-511e-b50f-1b0d65407b0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276319} {:answer/id #uuid "6f534c7a-cb11-5b8b-8823-ce6135e59b5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276478} {:answer/id #uuid "747d36d6-b304-566e-bae8-47d115a5aca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276531} {:answer/id #uuid "c346c257-8a91-52ac-af3f-17f228f8abce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "747d36d6-b304-566e-bae8-47d115a5aca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276531} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7281777-c50f-510c-980a-e03bcfd2b780" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "98673ae2-3448-5c6b-87b7-226f4a36e93c" :question/math-academy-id "q-158731" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{y}^{2}\\ge -2x},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{y}^{2}=-2x}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(-2,2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(-2,2)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(-1,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nThe set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or to the right of the parabola ${y}^{2}=-2x.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/e5/e5547cb8e0ed0fec1fd2e515a1bcd9009044289dfdc516c6a6cba14b650a7645.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(-2,2)$ and $Q(-1,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/7b/7b353dd43fd293424ba8404184e53869d41d8a1b73493d845bc85aa333130606.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(-2,2)$ satisfy the equation ${y}^{2}=-2x.$ Hence, $P$ lies on the boundary of the set.\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(-2,2)$ satisfy the inequality ${y}^{2}\\ge -2x.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278222 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124642 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06888019-59ee-5f1e-8518-4f07639a8da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275537} {:answer/id #uuid "4140e559-6092-5ae2-8bcd-a98f2357d749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276050} {:answer/id #uuid "42bb2cbf-5462-514b-b822-00b92a2500dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276065} {:answer/id #uuid "bc4265f7-2803-5a4c-af0b-23d6cfcd9d2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277152} {:answer/id #uuid "e710bfdf-fe8b-54d4-91db-e06b6c274c1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06888019-59ee-5f1e-8518-4f07639a8da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275537} :answer-field/id #uuid "53ce3a08-8352-57fb-9450-c1e8717cb404" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a6d4676b-36dc-5235-80c5-02c2a6d333fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-158733" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<9\\},\\,∂A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The point $P(1,2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n\nII. The point $P(1,2)$ belongs to $A$\n\nIII. The point $Q(3,3)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie inside the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/18/181a21e9975320e54365ef10bd801a2fa6494058dd40cda79cb29b713bd8df84.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,2)$ and $Q(3,3)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/15/15d42de861a3e687a269326e8338ea5d99b6a5a609f4a20e597e64ccc4a8474a.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of $P(1,2)$ do not satisfy the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(1,2)$ satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<9.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278261 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a5d11c2-4a7c-5fdc-a363-8a87b90db19e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0901f9cc-2e85-5737-94a9-7b639e5a3628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275555} {:answer/id #uuid "4ec853ad-ed6a-516c-8ae4-5a8f901436a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276177} {:answer/id #uuid "c86b1d1d-b0a5-5a0f-a4a7-754c482594b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277259} {:answer/id #uuid "d4cf432e-e934-591a-abcc-649994f1b071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277351} {:answer/id #uuid "de2694b0-4992-5426-80dd-680a5a3f1829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4cf432e-e934-591a-abcc-649994f1b071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277351} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5e236d5-38a4-59ff-bb41-dcbe9764411d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ffee0ea-a323-5b51-850e-98d4c4b547d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-159784" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w})=4$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}})=3$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ only. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$\n\n- Statement II is false. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. This means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ only. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w})=3$ (not $4$).\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}})=2$ (not $3$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}]}
